    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Few outdoor upgrades change the look of a home as quickly as a new driveway, and a paver driveway is one of the most attractive options. Unlike poured concrete or asphalt, it is a modular system made of individual units of stone, clay brick or concrete, laid over a carefully prepared base. In the UK this is usually called block paving, and you will see both terms used for the same thing.</p>
<p>Choosing a driveway surface affects your kerb appeal, how much upkeep you take on and how the front of your home works day to day. Below, we weigh the advantages of a paver driveway against its drawbacks so you can decide whether it is the right fit for your property.</p>

<h2 id="aesthetics">1. Aesthetics</h2>
<p>The most obvious benefit of a paver driveway is how good it looks. Instead of the single flat finish of asphalt or plain grey concrete, pavers offer a wide palette of colours, textures and shapes: classic clay bricks, rustic tumbled stone or crisp, modern concrete blocks.</p>
<p>The design possibilities are broad. You can choose patterns such as herringbone, basketweave or circular designs that become a focal point at the front of the house. Because pavers come in different sizes, you can also add borders and accent bands in a contrasting colour to frame the driveway, a level of detail that a poured surface cannot easily match. That flexibility lets you choose a driveway that suits the style of your home, whether it is a traditional brick terrace, a rustic cottage or a sharp contemporary build.</p>

<!--figure:paved-drive-to-brick-house|Pavers come in many colours and patterns to suit the style of the house.-->

<h2 id="durability">2. Durability</h2>
<p>A well-laid paver driveway is built to take everyday traffic. A solid concrete slab can crack under heavy loads or when the ground moves, whereas pavers form an interlocking, flexible surface. Because they are individual units, they can shift very slightly with the ground rather than cracking across the whole drive.</p>
<p>Good-quality concrete block paving made for driveways is designed to take vehicle loads; look for products rated for driveway or vehicular use rather than patio slabs. The joints between the units also help in areas with frequent freeze–thaw cycles, because they allow the surface to move a little instead of building up stress the way a continuous slab can.</p>

<h2 id="maintenance">3. Maintenance</h2>
<p>Pavers are hard-wearing, but they do need a regular maintenance routine to keep them looking their best. The main issue is the joints. Over time, the sand between the units can wash or sweep out, which lets weeds, moss and dirt settle in.</p>
<p>To keep on top of this:</p>
<ul>
<li>Top up the joint sand when you notice it dropping. Many homeowners use polymeric sand, which hardens when dampened and helps resist weeds; follow the product's instructions carefully.</li>
<li>Consider sealing the surface every few years, as your sealer manufacturer advises. A sealer can help protect against oil stains, fading and water penetration.</li>
<li>Sweep regularly and clean occasionally. If you use a pressure washer, choose a low setting and a fan nozzle, keep it moving and follow the paving manufacturer's guidance, as aggressive washing can strip sand from the joints.</li>
</ul>

<!--figure:weeds-at-paving-edge|Joints need topping up and occasional weeding to keep a paved driveway tidy.-->

<h2 id="cost">4. Cost</h2>
<p>For many homeowners, the biggest barrier is the initial cost. Paver driveways are usually more expensive to install than asphalt or poured concrete. Two things drive that: the price of the paving itself and the amount of labour involved.</p>
<p>Laying block paving is a meticulous process. It involves excavating the area, building and compacting a sub-base, screeding a bedding layer, placing each unit by hand, compacting the surface and filling the joints. That can take several days or longer, whereas a concrete driveway is often poured in a day once the groundwork is done. Against that, it helps to think about the long term: how long any driveway lasts depends on its installation, use, climate and maintenance, and a paver driveway's ability to be repaired in small sections can reduce the need for larger works later.</p>

<h2 id="repairs">5. Repairs</h2>
<p>One of the greatest practical advantages of block paving is that it is modular. If something heavy is dropped and cracks a block, or an oil leak stains a small patch, you do not need to replace the whole driveway. You lift the affected units and replace them with new ones.</p>
<p>The same applies to work underground. If a pipe or cable beneath a concrete or asphalt drive needs attention, the surface has to be broken out and patched, which often leaves a visible scar. With pavers, the blocks can be lifted carefully, the repair made and the same blocks relaid. Keep a few spare pavers from the original batch so any replacements match.</p>

<h2 id="installation">6. Installation</h2>
<p>A paver driveway is only as good as its installation. The sub-base is the most critical part: if the ground is not properly excavated and compacted, the pavers can sink, tilt or rut over time, creating trip hazards and puddles. Installers also set a gentle fall so that water drains away from the house, and fit firm edge restraints, such as concrete haunching or a purpose-made edging system, to stop the blocks creeping outwards.</p>
<p>A small path or a short drive can be a realistic DIY project for a confident, fit homeowner, but a full driveway is a heavy job. If you are weighing it up, our step-by-step guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-driveway">how to build a paver driveway</a> shows what is involved, and many people choose a professional for at least the excavation and base.</p>

<h2 id="climate">7. Climate</h2>
<p>Block paving copes well in a range of climates. In hot weather, lighter-coloured pavers tend to stay cooler underfoot than dark asphalt. Many pavers also have a textured surface that gives good grip in the wet, though smoother natural stone can be slipperier, so choose a finish suited to driveways.</p>
<p>For homes in areas with heavy rainfall, permeable block paving is worth considering. It lets water drain through wider joints and a specially designed base into the ground, which reduces runoff and puddling. In England, a permeable surface can also make a difference to whether you need planning permission for a front-garden driveway, so check with your local authority before you choose.</p>
<p>In winter, you can clear snow with a plastic shovel. For de-icers, use products the paving manufacturer or installer approves, and avoid rock salt on new paving.</p>

<h2 id="value">8. Value</h2>
<p>A paver driveway can give the front of a home strong kerb appeal, which shapes the first impression visitors and potential buyers have of the property. A neat, well-kept driveway can add appeal when you sell, but whether it adds value depends on your local market and the condition of the rest of the home, so treat it as a quality-of-life upgrade first rather than a guaranteed return.</p>

<h2 id="who-is-it-right-for">9. Who is it right for?</h2>
<p>A paver driveway is a good fit for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Long-term homeowners:</strong> if you plan to stay put, easy small-scale repairs and a durable surface make the higher upfront cost easier to justify.</li>
<li><strong>Design-conscious homeowners:</strong> anyone who wants the driveway to complement the architecture and planting at the front of the house.</li>
<li><strong>Eco-minded households:</strong> permeable block paving helps manage rainwater on site.</li>
<li><strong>Homes where finish matters:</strong> period properties and premium homes where a plain slab would look out of place.</li>
</ul>
<p>It may <strong>not</strong> suit you if you are on a tight budget right now, or if you want a surface that needs almost no attention; the joints will need some care. If cost is the main concern, read our guide to <a href="/blog/gravel-driveway-cost">what drives gravel driveway cost</a> as a comparison.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before you commit, lay a few sample pavers on the existing driveway and look at them in morning and evening light, wet and dry. Colours can look quite different outside than they do in a brochure or showroom.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Do paver driveways get weeds?</h3>
<p>Yes, weeds can grow in the joints if they are not maintained. Keeping the joints full, ideally with polymeric sand, and sweeping regularly greatly reduces the problem. If you use a weedkiller, follow the label and keep it away from drains, ponds and watercourses.</p>
<h3>How long does a paver driveway last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the quality of the installation, the base, the traffic it carries, your climate and how well it is maintained. A well-built driveway with a properly compacted sub-base should give many years of service, and damaged blocks can be replaced individually.</p>
<h3>Are pavers slippery when wet?</h3>
<p>Generally not, as most concrete pavers have a textured surface. Some smooth natural stones can be slipperier, so ask for a slip-resistant finish suitable for driveways.</p>
<h3>Can I use a snow blower on a paver driveway?</h3>
<p>Yes, but fit a plastic or rubber edge to the blade, or set it slightly above the surface, so it does not scratch the blocks or catch on raised edges.</p>
<h3>Is a paver driveway more expensive than concrete?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes, because of the materials and the hand labour. The final cost depends on the paver type, the pattern, the area, access and how much groundwork your site needs, so get itemised quotes based on the same specification.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A paver driveway combines strength with real design flexibility, as long as it sits on a well-built base and you are happy to look after the joints. If you are ready to plan one, read <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-driveway">how to build a paver driveway</a>, and if you are still comparing surfaces, see <a href="/blog/concrete-driveway-mistakes">common concrete driveway mistakes</a>. For help tying the driveway into the rest of your front garden, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
